# Static Code Review Report

| Line Number | Defect Type | Why It Is a Problem | Proposed Fix | Severity |
| :---: | :--- | :--- | :--- | :---: |
| **40** | **Buffer Overflow (Array Bounds)** | `spawnMonster` does not check if `monsterCount` reached `MAX_MONSTERS` (5). Adding a 6th monster in `main()` causes out-of-bounds array writing and memory corruption. | Add check: `if (monsterCount >= MAX_MONSTERS) return;` or use `std::vector<Monster*>`. | **High** |
| **46** | **Assignment instead of Comparison** | `monsters[i]->id = id` mutates the monster's ID to `id` and always evaluates to `true` (unless `id == 0`), returning the wrong monster every time. | Change to equality check: `if (monsters[i]->id == id)`. | **High** |
| **53–54** | **Null Pointer Dereference** | If `findMonster(id)` returns `nullptr` (e.g., ID 99), dereferencing `m->health` causes a segmentation fault / crash. | Add check: `if (!m) return 0;`. | **High** |
| **55** | **Unsigned Arithmetic Underflow / Logic Failure** | `dmg` is `unsigned int`. `m->health - dmg` promotes to `unsigned`, which underflows to a massive positive number when health < damage, making `< 0` evaluate to `false`. | Compare directly: `if (m->health <= damage) return 0;`. | **High** |
| **63** | **Off-By-One / Out-of-Bounds Read** | Loop uses `i <= monsterCount` instead of `i < monsterCount`. Accesses uninitialized index `monsters[monsterCount]`, causing undefined behavior or crash. | Change loop condition to `for (int i = 0; i < monsterCount; i++)`. | **High** |
| **70–73** | **Dangling Reference to Local Variable & Null Dereference** | 1. Dereferences `m` without checking for `nullptr`.<br>2. Returns `std::string&` referring to local variable `label`, which is destroyed when function returns, causing undefined behavior. | Return string by value (`std::string`) and add `nullptr` check for `m`. | **High** |
| **77, 80** | **Uninitialized Variable & Division By Zero** | `double sum` is uninitialized, so it contains garbage data resulting in invalid total. Also, if `monsterCount == 0`, division by zero occurs. | Initialize `double sum = 0.0;` and check `if (monsterCount == 0) return 0;`. | **High** |
| **85–86** | **Use-After-Free & Null Dereference** | `delete m;` deallocates memory first, then `m->name` accesses freed memory on line 86. Also lacks `nullptr` check. | Print name before `delete`: `std::cout << "Defeated " << m->name << "\n"; delete m;`. Also update/re-index array. | **High** |
| **91** | **Integer Truncation in Division** | `attack * armorPercent` performs integer arithmetic before dividing by `100`. Loss of precision occurs before returning `double`. | Cast to floating point: `return (attack * static_cast<double>(armorPercent)) / 100.0;`. | **Low** |
| **98** | **Missing Return Value Path** | If `score <= 100` (e.g., `score = 50` in `main`), function exits without returning a value, causing undefined behavior. | Add default return: `else return 3;` or `return 0;`. | **Medium** |
| **102, 105** | **Buffer Overflow & Mismatched Deallocation** | `cin >> name` into 10-byte buffer allows buffer overflow. `delete name` causes undefined behavior because `new[]` requires `delete[]`. | Use `std::string name;` instead of raw dynamic char array and `cin >> name;`. | **High** |
| **118** | **Unmanaged Memory Leak** | `Monster* bonus` allocated with `new` on heap in `main()` is never freed with `delete`. | Call `delete bonus;` before function exit or use smart pointers. | **Medium** |
| **129** | **Shallow Copy / Double Free** | `Game` lacks custom Copy Constructor and Copy Assignment operator. Default shallow copy duplicates raw pointers. Destructors run twice for same memory on exit. | Implement Deep Copy Constructor/Assignment Operator, or disable copying (`Game(const Game&) = delete;`). | **High** |
